Unit 4 unfolds across four compelling lectures, each delving into intricate aspects of differential equations, showcasing a blend of theoretical exposition and practical application.
Lecture 29 begins the unit with an exploration of higher-order linear equations, introducing operator notation to simplify complex mathematical operations. It establishes the foundation of linear differential operators, demonstrating their application through examples. The lecture progresses to elucidate the concept of the Wronskian, a determinant used to determine the linear independence of functions, and discusses the structure and solutions of higher-order linear homogeneous ordinary differential equations (ODEs) with constant coefficients, including distinct real roots, complex roots, and repeated real roots, offering a methodical approach to solving these equations.
Lecture 30 shifts focus to systems of differential equations, emphasizing the significance of distinct eigenvalues. It delineates the process of finding eigenvalues and eigenvectors for a 2x2 system and extends these concepts to tackle real, distinct eigenvalues. Through illustrative examples, the lecture outlines the steps to determine the general solution of such systems, introducing a practical application of eigenvalues and eigenvectors in forming these solutions.
Lecture 31 addresses complex roots within the context of constant coefficient homogeneous systems. It presents a theorem for constructing a fundamental set of real solutions when the system exhibits complex roots, elucidating this with examples that demonstrate how to find the eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and ultimately, the general solution to systems characterized by complex roots.
Lecture 32 concludes the unit by exploring systems of differential equations with repeated eigenvalues. It provides a theoretical framework for understanding the implications of repeated eigenvalues on the system’s behavior and outlines a structured approach to finding the general solution, incorporating both theoretical insight and practical examples.
Collectively, these lectures offer a comprehensive overview of solving higher-order differential equations and systems of equations, emphasizing the role of eigenvalues and eigenvectors in uncovering the dynamics of these systems. Through a blend of theory and examples, Unit 4 equips students with the knowledge and tools necessary to tackle complex differential equations, fostering a deeper understanding of their applications in various fields.

3.5 Using matlab
% Define the matrix A
A = [4 -5; 5 -2];
disp('Matrix A:');
disp(A);

% Calculate the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors
[V, D] = eig(A);

% Eigenvalues (stored diagonally in D)
disp('Eigenvalues:');
disp(diag(D));

% Eigenvectors (stored as columns in V)
disp('Eigenvectors:');
disp(V);
